      <h2>Airlines Flying Nonstop from Toronto to New York</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Air Canada</strong> — Multiple daily flights from YYZ to JFK, LGA, and EWR</li>
        <li><strong>Porter Airlines</strong> — Multiple daily flights from Billy Bishop (YTZ) to Newark (EWR) — a highly convenient city-centre to city-centre option</li>
        <li><strong>American Airlines</strong> — Daily service from YYZ to JFK and LGA</li>
        <li><strong>Delta Air Lines</strong> — Daily service from YYZ to JFK and LGA</li>
        <li><strong>United Airlines</strong> — Daily service from YYZ to EWR</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>Porter Airlines: The City-Centre Option</h2>
      <p>Porter Airlines operates from Billy Bishop Toronto City Airport (YTZ) — a small island airport accessible by ferry right from downtown Toronto — to Newark Liberty (EWR), which connects to Manhattan via AirTrain and NJ Transit. For travelers staying in downtown Toronto or Manhattan, this is often the most convenient door-to-door option.</p>
      <div class="info-box"><p>ℹ️ Porter flights are popular with business travelers specifically because of the downtown airport convenience. Book early — Porter's YTZ–EWR routes sell out quickly, particularly on Monday morning and Friday evening departures.</p></div>
      <h2>Tips for Toronto to New York Nonstop</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>The short flight time (1.5 hours) makes this a natural day-trip or overnight-trip destination from either city.</li>
        <li>NEXUS card holders have expedited customs at Pearson — sign up if you cross the border regularly.</li>
        <li>Air Canada's Aeroplan miles have poor redemption value on short-haul routes — consider cash fares or Porter for this corridor.</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>Terminal Guide: Toronto and New York</h2>
      <p><strong>At Toronto Pearson International (YYZ):</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Terminal 1 (T1):</strong> Air Canada's home terminal. All AC nonstop to New York departs from T1. US-bound flights clear US Customs and Border Protection (CBP) preclearance at YYZ before boarding — this is a major benefit of flying from Pearson, as you arrive in the US as a domestic arrival with no US customs to clear on landing.</li>
        <li><strong>Terminal 3 (T3):</strong> American Airlines, Delta, and United operate from T3 at YYZ. T3 also has US CBP preclearance for US-bound flights.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>At Toronto Billy Bishop City Airport (YTZ):</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li>Porter Airlines operates exclusively from YTZ. The airport is accessed via the pedestrian tunnel from the mainland (Eireann Quay) or by ferry — both are free and take under 5 minutes. YTZ also has US CBP preclearance for Porter flights to Newark, meaning you clear US customs in Toronto before departure.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>At New York JFK:</strong> Air Canada, American Airlines, and Delta use their respective terminals (T2/T8 for AC/AA, T4 for Delta). Because US preclearance is done in Toronto, you arrive as a domestic passenger and use domestic baggage claim.</p>
      <p><strong>At Newark Liberty (EWR):</strong> Porter uses Terminal A. United uses Terminal C. Both connect to the AirTrain for NJ Transit rail service to Penn Station Manhattan (~30 minutes) or direct taxi/rideshare.</p>
      <div class="tip-box"><p>💡 US Customs preclearance at YYZ and YTZ is one of the biggest underrated advantages of flying from Toronto to New York. You arrive at JFK or EWR as a domestic passenger — no US customs queue on arrival, faster exit from the airport, and access to domestic baggage carousels.</p></div>
      <h2>Baggage Allowances by Airline</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Air Canada:</strong> Carry-on and personal item included on all fares. First checked bag $30–35 CAD on Tango (basic) fares. Included on Flex and above. Latitude and Business Class include multiple bags.</li>
        <li><strong>Porter Airlines:</strong> One carry-on and personal item included on all Porter fares. First checked bag included on PorterReserve and above; $30 CAD on PorterClassic. Porter's generous carry-on policy (no size fees on standard bags) is distinctive for a value carrier.</li>
        <li><strong>American/Delta/United:</strong> Same structure as domestic US — first checked bag $35 USD each way unless you hold status or a co-branded credit card.</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>Best Time to Book Toronto to New York Nonstop</h2>
      <p><strong>This route is price-stable year-round</strong> given the strength of business demand between Canada's largest city and the US financial capital. Fares rarely drop as dramatically as pure leisure routes. Business-day departures (Monday morning, Thursday/Friday evening) command premiums.</p>
      <p><strong>Best value:</strong> Tuesday, Wednesday, and Saturday departures. Mid-January through February. Early September. These windows yield the most competitive fares on this short-haul corridor.</p>
      <p><strong>Most expensive:</strong> Labour Day weekend. Thanksgiving (both Canadian in October and US in November). Christmas and New Year's. TIFF (Toronto International Film Festival, September) creates unusual demand from YYZ.</p>
      <h2>Entry Requirements and Border Crossing</h2>
      <p><strong>Canadian citizens entering the US:</strong> Canadian passport holders do not require a US visa for tourism or business stays under 6 months. Complete the US Customs and Border Protection form at preclearance in Toronto before boarding. A valid Canadian passport is required — driver's licence alone is not sufficient for air travel.</p>
      <p><strong>US citizens entering Canada on return:</strong> US citizens do not require a visa for Canada. A valid US passport is required. Canadian CBSA (Border Services Agency) processes arrivals at YYZ — typically fast with e-passport kiosks available.</p>
      <p><strong>NEXUS card holders:</strong> NEXUS card holders use dedicated preclearance lanes at both YYZ and YTZ, bypassing standard CBP queues. If you regularly cross the US-Canada border by air, NEXUS ($50 CAD, valid 5 years) is worth the application effort for the time savings alone.</p>
      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      <h3>Is the Porter Billy Bishop to Newark route worth the downtown convenience?</h3>
      <p>For travelers based in downtown Toronto heading to Manhattan, yes — strongly. The subway-to-ferry-to-gate process from King Street is often faster than driving to Pearson in traffic. The Newark arrival connects to Penn Station in 30 minutes via NJ Transit. The total door-to-door time from downtown Toronto to midtown Manhattan can be under 3 hours on a good day. Porter's service quality and inclusive carry-on policy are also advantages.</p>
      <h3>What are the check-in times at Billy Bishop (YTZ)?</h3>
      <p>Porter recommends arriving 90 minutes before departure at YTZ for US flights (to allow time for CBP preclearance). The airport itself is tiny and check-in takes minutes — but the CBP queue can extend this. During peak morning departures (7am–9am), allow the full 90 minutes.</p>
      <h3>Can I use Aeroplan miles for Toronto–New York?</h3>
      <p>Yes, but at poor value. Short-haul Aeroplan awards cost 6,000 points each way in economy — reasonable on paper, but these points are worth far more on long-haul redemptions. Unless you have surplus Aeroplan points about to expire, cash fares on this short route are typically better value for your miles.</p>
      <h3>How does US CBP preclearance work at Toronto airports?</h3>
      <p>US Customs and Border Protection operates a full preclearance facility at both YYZ and YTZ. Before boarding your flight to the US, you proceed through CBP screening — presenting your passport, customs declaration, and answering officer questions — exactly as you would on arrival in the US. Once cleared, you board as a domestic passenger. On arrival at JFK, EWR, or other US airports, you proceed directly to domestic baggage claim and exit — no further customs processing required.</p>
      <h2>In-Flight Experience</h2>
      <p>This is a 90-minute nonstop — in-flight service is minimal regardless of carrier. The main differentiator is seat comfort and carry-on policy. Air Canada's Airbus A320 family offers standard economy seats with reasonable pitch. Porter's aircraft (Embraer E195-E2) are configured with 2-2 seating — no middle seats — which is a distinctive advantage for solo travelers who would otherwise be assigned a middle seat on narrower aircraft.</p>
